From 29e97b6f976a9fc143a419d996bb580dc0fb7c99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:52:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] ignore annotations needing the size of an incomplete
 type

For an incomplete non-void, non-function type (opaque struct or union,
unsized array, or forward-declared enum), E-ACSL emitted sizeof or
__alignof__ of the incomplete type into the instrumented code, which
does not compile:
- \valid(obj), \valid_read(obj) and \initialized(obj) use the size of
  the pointed type (term_to_ptr_and_size, range_to_ptr_and_size);
- the RTE assertion \aligned(p, alignof(T)) generated for a cast to
  T * translates alignof(T) as is;
- user-written sizeof(T), alignof(T), and expression forms sizeof(e)
  (TSizeOfE) or TAlignOfE on an expression of incomplete type emit
  sizeof/__alignof__ of that incomplete type.
E-ACSL already checks completeness for its own alignment guards (and
warns instead), but not for these terms.

Reject TSizeOf, TAlignOf, TSizeOfE and TAlignOfE of an incomplete
non-void, non-function type in typing, and memory predicates whose
pointee is incomplete in memory_translate, before any statement is
generated. The annotation is ignored with the usual "not yet
supported" warning. void and function types are left out: sizeof(void)
is accepted by gcc and clang, so \valid on a void * keeps working.

Add constructs/incomplete_type.c as a regression test.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
